// Topic-specific explanations recovered from the original guides' subject coverage.
// Numerical examples are hypothetical; institution rankings and unsourced forecasts
// from the original are not carried into these explanations.
export const GUIDE_DETAILS = {
  'loans/home-loan-true-cost': [
    ['Build a cost ledger without counting the same expense twice',
      'Separate the purchase budget from the borrowing budget. The down payment, stamp duty, registration and furnishing belong to acquiring and occupying the property; loan interest and lender charges belong to financing it. Both affect affordability, but adding the full property price to every EMI would count the financed principal twice. A useful ledger shows cash needed before possession, payments during construction and repayments after regular instalments begin.',
      'For a hypothetical ₹50 lakh loan at 8.75% for 240 months, the monthly reducing-balance EMI is approximately ₹44,186. Scheduled repayments are about ₹1.0605 crore, made up of the ₹50 lakh principal and about ₹56.05 lakh interest. That total already includes repayment of the principal. Add only separate charges that were not financed or already included in those payments. If an insurance premium is added to the loan balance, recalculate the EMI instead of counting the same premium as both an upfront payment and additional principal.',
      'Keep a separate column for the date each expense must be paid. A ₹25,000 processing bill before disbursement and the same amount spread across later instalments have different cash-flow consequences. Ask whether an application-stage fee is refundable if the property is rejected, the sanctioned amount is lower than requested or you decide not to take the loan. A low quoted rate is less useful if an early non-refundable payment forces a decision before the offer is complete.'],
    ['Compare processing, legal, valuation and mortgage charges',
      'An advertised processing fee may be a percentage subject to a minimum or maximum, a flat amount, or a promotional waiver. For an illustrative 0.5% charge on ₹50 lakh, the fee is ₹25,000 before any applicable tax; this is arithmetic, not a bank tariff. Check the taxable base and invoice rather than applying one percentage to every property or government charge. Legal scrutiny, technical valuation and documentation can be separate lines.',
      'Mortgage creation or registration expenses are also distinct from stamp duty on the property purchase. The applicable state, type of security, property value and process can affect the amount. Request the name of each charge, who collects it and the document it relates to. This makes it possible to compare two lender quotes without treating a missing line as a saving.',
      'Do not assume a bank has validated every aspect of a property for the buyer. Lending approval, valuation and the purchaser’s own title and contract review serve different purposes. Allow time for independent scrutiny of the sale agreement, completion status and payment milestones before making non-refundable commitments.'],
    ['Why the early EMIs contain so much interest',
      'Each monthly interest amount is calculated on the opening outstanding under the loan’s method. In the simplified ₹50 lakh, 8.75% example, first-month interest is about ₹36,458; approximately ₹7,728 of the ₹44,186 EMI reduces principal. As the balance falls, the interest portion falls and more of the same EMI repays principal. A precise schedule should calculate the changing balance, not draw a chart using an arbitrary percentage for each year.',
      'This explains why an early part-payment can affect more future interest than the same payment made close to maturity, if other terms remain unchanged. The saving still depends on the rate path and whether the lender reduces the EMI or the tenure. A shorter tenure at the existing EMI and a lower EMI over the existing tenure do not produce the same total interest. Ask for the revised schedule and compare both outcomes.',
      'Prepaying is not automatically the best use of every spare rupee. Retain money needed for near-term expenses and emergencies. Compare the avoided borrowing cost with a realistic after-tax alternative, and consider charges and any actual deduction you qualify for. Do not use an investment return forecast as a guaranteed offset to a contractual loan cost.'],
    ['Model construction-stage interest and delayed possession',
      'For staged disbursement, interest can start on money the lender has already released even though the entire sanctioned amount has not been drawn. In a hypothetical simple monthly illustration, ₹10 lakh outstanding at 9% annual interest means ₹7,500 monthly interest; ₹30 lakh at the same rate means ₹22,500. Paying this interest alone does not reduce the disbursed principal. The agreement determines when full instalments or principal repayment start.',
      'Build a timeline with the booking payment, each construction demand, lender disbursement, rent and interest payment. Test a delay scenario without assuming the developer reimburses it. If possession is postponed while rent and pre-EMI continue, a household can face two housing-related outflows even before regular mortgage repayment begins. Include only the actual dates and amounts in a final comparison.',
      'If an offer promises deferred payments or an interest subsidy, identify who pays the interest, for how long and what happens if that party fails to pay. Read the lender agreement separately from the developer’s sales promise. Check whether deferred interest is later added to principal and how the repayment schedule changes.'],
    ['Negotiate the terms that change the total cost',
      'Use competing written offers for the same amount, tenure and borrower profile. Ask the lender to explain the benchmark, spread, reset frequency, processing charge and any conversion fee. A useful request is: “Please show my offered rate and spread, all upfront charges, and the repayment schedule for the same ₹50 lakh and 20-year term.” This produces comparable evidence instead of a verbal best-rate assurance.',
      'For a balance transfer, compare remaining interest under both offers after legal, valuation, mortgage and closure-related costs. Restarting a long tenure can lower the EMI while increasing total repayment. Check the outstanding at the proposed transfer date rather than using the original sanctioned amount. A simple fee-divided-by-EMI-saving calculation is a preliminary screen, not a complete comparison of loans whose principal schedules differ.',
      'Tax savings should enter the ledger only after the relevant year, regime, property use and eligibility are identified. The same interest payment does not create the same deduction for every borrower. Keep principal, interest and certificates separate, and use the applicable tax rules rather than adding every historically advertised home-loan deduction together.']
  ],
  'loans/personal-loan-actual-apr': [
    ['Flat rate and reducing-balance rate measure different things',
      'Under a flat-rate illustration, interest is calculated on the starting principal for the whole stated term even as instalments repay it. Under a reducing-balance calculation, each period’s interest uses the outstanding balance. Two loans both advertised with the same percentage can therefore have different repayment amounts. Ask which method applies before comparing the headline number.',
      'For a hypothetical ₹1 lakh over two years at a 10% flat annual rate, total interest is ₹20,000 and equal monthly repayment would be ₹5,000 before fees. At a 10% nominal annual reducing-balance rate, the 24-month EMI is about ₹4,614 and total interest is about ₹10,748. Neither is a lender quote. They demonstrate why the word “flat” cannot be ignored.'],
    ['Reconstruct the cash you receive and the cash you repay',
      'Start with the amount credited to your account after mandatory deductions, not only the sanctioned amount. List instalments by date and add required charges paid separately. If a processing fee is deducted from the disbursal, count it there; do not add it a second time as another upfront payment. If a fee is financed, it changes the balance and repayment obligation.',
      'The rate equating net disbursal with the present value of the repayments provides a cash-flow comparison. APR disclosure follows the applicable methodology, so use the lender’s Key Facts Statement and amortisation schedule rather than calling any fee-adjusted percentage an official APR. Optional products need a separate, clearly identified line in your personal budget.'],
    ['A longer tenure can conceal an expensive offer',
      'Compare offers at both the same amount and the same tenure. A smaller EMI obtained by extending repayment from three years to five years does not establish a cheaper loan. Look at total scheduled repayment, the outstanding after a year and the cost of repaying early if that is your plan.',
      'Test whether the instalment remains affordable after rent, existing debts, regular expenses and a reasonable contingency. Approval tells you that a lender has accepted the application; it does not prove that taking the maximum offered amount serves your needs. Borrow the amount required for the stated purpose instead of automatically accepting a larger pre-approved limit.'],
    ['Compare offers before negotiating or refinancing',
      'Ask for a written offer showing disbursal, EMI, term, rate method, APR, fees, insurance choice and part-payment rules. Use the same borrower details across lenders, and distinguish a soft eligibility indication from a final sanctioned quote. A starting rate reserved for selected profiles should not be treated as your rate.',
      'When refinancing, add the new processing cost and any legitimate closure cost to the comparison. Use the actual outstanding balance and remaining term. A new loan that lowers this month’s EMI by restarting a long term may increase the amount you repay overall. Get a settlement statement for the old loan and verify closure after the transfer.']
  ],
  'loans/fixed-vs-floating-rate': [
    ['Identify what is actually fixed',
      'A fixed-rate label may describe the full term, a limited introductory period or a contract with a reset clause. Read the length of the fixed period, what happens afterwards and whether a change is permitted under specified conditions. A hybrid product can begin fixed and later move to a benchmark-linked rate; compare both phases.',
      'For a floating loan, record the benchmark, spread, reset frequency and effective dates. A benchmark movement does not necessarily change your next payment on the same day. The account’s reset rules determine timing. Separate a lender changing its published offer for new customers from a change to your existing contractual spread.'],
    ['Stress-test the remaining loan rather than predicting rates',
      'Model the outstanding at the reset date, the remaining tenure and the revised rate. For a hypothetical ₹20 lakh balance with ten years remaining, a constant 8% rate gives an EMI of about ₹24,266; 9% gives about ₹25,335. This ₹1,069 monthly difference is an illustration of sensitivity, not a forecast or bank quote.',
      'Ask what happens if you keep the existing EMI: the tenure may change instead. Then ask what happens if you keep the remaining tenure: the EMI may change. A model that changes both without saying so is difficult to interpret. Check affordability under a higher-rate scenario before relying on a favourable future decline.'],
    ['Measure the price of payment certainty',
      'A fixed offer can provide predictable scheduled payments, but compare its initial rate and fees with the floating alternative. In a hypothetical comparison, paying ₹1,000 more per month for twelve months costs ₹12,000 before any other difference. Whether that certainty is worth the cost depends on your budget, remaining tenure and ability to absorb resets.',
      'There is no universal break-even rate path. A floating rate that rises early and falls late can produce a different total from one with the same average rate in a different order, because the outstanding balance changes. Compare complete schedules and use scenarios instead of declaring one rate type always cheaper.'],
    ['Check switching and repayment flexibility',
      'Ask about conversion eligibility, fees and whether the offered spread changes when switching. Obtain the revised terms before accepting. A conversion fee is recovered only if future savings exceed it over the period you actually keep the loan.',
      'Read prepayment and closure rules for the actual rate type and loan category. A fixed or hybrid product may not have the same treatment as a floating loan. Keep records of reset notices and updated schedules so you can see whether the lender changed the EMI, tenure or both.']
  ],
  'loans/sbi-vs-hdfc-vs-icici-home-loan': [
    ['Compare three personalised quotes on one worksheet',
      'SBI, HDFC Bank and ICICI Bank can quote different rates to different borrowers and properties. Build the comparison from written offers for the same principal, tenure, rate type and disbursement plan. Record the quote date and expiry. A published starting rate and another lender’s final sanction are not comparable evidence.',
      'For each offer, enter benchmark, spread, reset cycle, initial EMI, scheduled interest, processing fee, legal and valuation expenses, mortgage-related charges and optional products. Mark missing information as a question rather than zero. If a fee is waived, record the channel and conditions that make the waiver apply.'],
    ['Processing and property acceptance can affect the decision',
      'A lender’s treatment of a particular property, income pattern or co-borrower can matter as much as a small rate difference. Ask what documents remain outstanding, whether the property has passed legal and technical checks, and what conditions apply before disbursement. A tentative approval is different from funds ready for release.',
      'Compare your actual purchase deadline with the lender’s stated next steps. Do not invent a universal approval time for a bank. Keep communication about document completion and release dates in writing, particularly for a seller’s payment deadline or construction-linked demand.'],
    ['Evaluate servicing after the first disbursement',
      'Ask how to obtain statements, interest certificates, a revised repayment schedule and a foreclosure quote. For staged loans, check how later construction demands are processed. For a floating rate, understand how notices arrive and how to request an EMI or tenure change.',
      'Digital access, branch convenience and support quality affect the effort of managing a long loan, but personal preference should be recorded separately from calculated savings. A slightly cheaper quote can still be unsuitable if its disbursement conditions do not fit the transaction.'],
    ['Make the negotiation specific',
      'Instead of asking only for the lowest rate, give the lender a comparable written quote and ask which rate, spread or fee it can match. Request the revised terms together: “If the rate changes, please confirm the processing fee, benchmark spread and waiver conditions still apply.” A verbal concession on one line may hide a change elsewhere.',
      'Choose the offer with the best complete fit for your expected holding period and repayment plan. If you expect an early part-payment or transfer, include the applicable process and costs. Avoid declaring one bank the cheapest nationally when the available evidence consists of one borrower’s offers.']
  ],
  'loans/prepayment-penalty-trap': [
    ['Part-payment, foreclosure and an advance EMI are different',
      'A part-payment reduces principal while the loan stays open. Foreclosure closes the remaining obligation. An advance instalment can instead sit as a future payment unless it is applied to principal under the lender’s process. Before sending money, ask how it will be booked and obtain an acknowledgement showing the revised outstanding.',
      'Read any minimum part-payment, frequency, notice and settlement-date conditions. Do not assume every loan has a lock-in or that none can have charges. Rate type, purpose, lender category, sanction date and the applicable rules matter. The loan’s Key Facts Statement and agreement should identify the terms relevant to the account.'],
    ['Compare reducing the EMI with reducing the tenure',
      'After an identical principal reduction, keeping the EMI and shortening the remaining term generally pays down the loan faster in an unchanged-rate model. Keeping the term and reducing the EMI provides monthly budget relief. Those are different objectives; ask the lender for both revised schedules before choosing.',
      'Record the payment date, balance immediately before it, charge if applicable and balance after it. Future interest saving should be measured against the no-prepayment schedule from that same date. Do not compare a revised loan with the original total interest as if every earlier instalment could now be avoided.'],
    ['Keep emergency liquidity in the comparison',
      'Prepaying converts liquid savings into a lower debt balance. It can reduce future interest but may leave less money available for a medical bill, job interruption or essential repair. A later loan to replace that liquidity can cost more than the interest initially saved.',
      'Compare a realistic alternative after tax and risk, and account for any actual tax deduction on loan interest. Avoid assuming that a volatile investment will earn a fixed future return. Use your cash-flow needs and borrowing cost to decide how much to prepay rather than treating every bonus as an automatic foreclosure payment.'],
    ['Verify closure and the return of security documents',
      'Request a dated settlement quote and follow the lender’s payment instructions. After payment, obtain the closure or no-dues confirmation, final account statement and any security-release documents relevant to the loan. Track the process for returning original property documents when the loan is secured.',
      'A payment receipt alone does not establish that the account is closed. Check for a remaining interest or fee entry and confirm that an old repayment mandate is handled correctly. Retain the settlement and closure records for future disputes or credit-report corrections.']
  ],
  'loans/education-loan-real-cost': [
    ['Draw a disbursement and study-period timeline',
      'Education loans often release money against tuition or other approved demands rather than providing the whole sanctioned amount on day one. List each release, its currency where relevant and the date interest begins on it. Living expenses, travel, equipment and course deposits need their own budget lines if they are not financed.',
      'A moratorium usually concerns repayment timing; it should not be assumed to mean interest-free study. Read whether interest accrues, whether it must be serviced and when unpaid interest is added to the balance. A government subsidy or lender concession has separate eligibility and documentation requirements.'],
    ['Capitalisation changes the amount entering repayment',
      'In a simplified illustration, a fully disbursed ₹10 lakh balance at 10% simple annual interest for two years adds ₹2 lakh if none of that interest is paid. If the contract capitalises that accrued amount at repayment start, the opening repayment balance becomes ₹12 lakh. Actual staged releases and the lender’s accrual method change the result.',
      'Paying study-period interest can reduce the amount later financed, but only if your family can afford the outflow. Compare monthly interest servicing with the amount added under the actual schedule. Do not describe a fixed rupee saving without specifying disbursal dates, interest rate and repayment term.'],
    ['Test the repayment against a conservative starting income',
      'Use plausible take-home income after essential living costs, tax and relocation expenses. A course’s highest advertised placement salary is not a reliable repayment budget for every student. Consider a later start date or lower starting salary and check what happens to the loan if employment is delayed.',
      'A longer term reduces the scheduled monthly burden but can increase total interest. Compare the outstanding after two or three years and what a planned part-payment would change. Keep the course’s educational and career value separate from a promise that debt will pay for itself.'],
    ['Check currency, co-borrower and tax details',
      'For overseas study, distinguish the rupee loan from foreign-currency tuition and living costs. An exchange-rate change can increase the rupee amount needed before a later disbursement. Transfer charges and university deadlines can also affect the usable amount. Build a contingency rather than assuming every semester costs the first semester’s rupee amount.',
      'Review co-borrower obligations, collateral conditions, insurance choice and the documents required for each release. An interest deduction, if available under the relevant year and regime, is separate from loan approval and is not a deduction for every principal repayment. Keep the lender’s annual interest certificate and match it to the applicable filing rules.']
  ],
  'insurance/health-insurance-effective-coverage': [
    ['Follow a hospital bill through the policy clauses',
      'Begin with the billed amount and identify which expenses fall within the coverage definition. Then locate applicable treatment limits, room conditions, deductible, co-payment and remaining cover. These are different deductions, and their order is determined by the wording. Applying every percentage to the entire bill can overstate the reduction just as ignoring them can overstate the payout.',
      'Suppose a hypothetical ₹3 lakh bill contains ₹20,000 of expenses excluded under the policy. The admissible amount before cost sharing is ₹2.8 lakh. If that imaginary policy then applies only a 20% co-payment to that amount, the patient’s co-payment is ₹56,000 and the insurer’s amount is ₹2.24 lakh, assuming adequate available cover and no other deductions. Total patient spending is ₹76,000, including the excluded ₹20,000. This is an example of the stated assumptions, not a prediction for any actual insurer.',
      'If a deductible or room-linked adjustment is also present, do not just add its headline percentage to 20%. Read what base it applies to and ask for an itemised calculation. The sum insured is an upper boundary under the policy, not a promise that every bill up to that amount is admissible.'],
    ['Room entitlement affects more than comfort',
      'Read whether the entitlement is a room category, a monetary daily cap or another definition. Check ICU separately. If a hospital prices services differently by room, moving to a higher category may affect related charges. A proportional-deduction clause must be read for its actual scope; some costs may be treated differently.',
      'Before planned admission, ask the hospital which room satisfies the policy and request the estimated bill for it. Ask what happens if that category is unavailable, and obtain the insurer’s response rather than assuming an exception. Keep admission and room-allocation records. A “no room-rent cap” feature still does not settle exclusions, treatment sub-limits or co-payment.'],
    ['Waiting periods and disclosure are separate from the cover amount',
      'A large sum insured does not remove a waiting-period condition for a specified illness or treatment. Record the policy start date, continuity, relevant waiting period and any endorsement or exclusion. When buying, answer health questions accurately and retain the submitted proposal, including disclosures made through an intermediary.',
      'If increasing cover, ask how waiting-period treatment differs between existing cover and the additional amount. If changing insurer, review the portability or migration process and what continuity credit is accepted. Do not assume that cancelling an existing policy before the replacement is issued preserves uninterrupted protection.'],
    ['Family floaters, restoration and bonuses need context',
      'In a family floater, several insured people may share one cover pool. Model more than one admission in a year, rather than valuing the headline sum independently for every family member. Ask how the remaining sum changes after the first claim and whether any restoration can apply to the same person or illness.',
      'A restoration feature may have trigger, usage and timing conditions; it is not automatically unlimited fresh cover for the first claim. A cumulative bonus can change on renewal or after claims according to the contract. Separate base cover, bonus, restored cover and any top-up in your worksheet so that you do not count a conditional amount as always available.'],
    ['Cashless, reimbursement and claim records',
      'Cashless treatment is a settlement arrangement with a participating provider, not a waiver of policy conditions. Initial authorisation can differ from final approval. Ask the hospital which expenses the insurer has approved and which deposits or non-covered amounts you may need to pay. Save the authorisation and the final settlement detail.',
      'For reimbursement, retain the documents required by the policy and insurer, including invoices, discharge records, investigations and payment evidence. Submit within the applicable process and keep a record of requests for additional information. If there is a deduction, request its clause, amount and calculation; then use the grievance process for a disputed decision.',
      'For a family choosing cover, compare the hospitals actually used, room entitlement, relevant waiting periods, cost sharing and likely out-of-pocket exposure. A cheap premium and a large printed cover are incomplete comparison criteria. Ask for an illustration on the same hospital scenario under each shortlisted policy and record the assumptions.']
  ],
  'insurance/claim-settlement-ratios-2026': [
    ['Identify the numerator and denominator',
      'A ratio by number of claims answers a different question from a ratio by amount. In a hypothetical example, settling 95 out of 100 claims gives a 95% count ratio. If the unsettled claims account for a large part of the amounts claimed, an amount-based measure can be much lower. Neither measure alone explains the reasons for those outcomes.',
      'Record the reporting period, insurance business, measure definition and source table. A group-health portfolio and retail individual policies can have different claim patterns. Do not compare a life-insurance death-claim measure with a health-insurance incurred-claims measure simply because both are percentages.'],
    ['Pending, rejected and paid claims are different states',
      'Check how the report treats pending claims and whether opening pending cases carry into the period. A claim not paid by the reporting cut-off is not automatically a final rejection. The measurement date and settlement timeline can affect a ratio even where the underlying policy rules are unchanged.',
      'Also distinguish the number of claims paid from the portion of each hospital bill admitted. A paid health claim can still involve exclusions or cost sharing. An insurer-level settlement count therefore cannot be converted into a percentage of your own future bill that will be reimbursed.'],
    ['Use portfolio data alongside the policy you will buy',
      'Read the exact plan’s room terms, waiting periods, sub-limits, co-payment and exclusions. Check the hospitals available near you and the process for a planned admission. A favourable portfolio ratio cannot remove a restriction in a contract, and a low premium cannot compensate for cover that excludes the treatment you need.',
      'Service measures, complaints and turnaround data can add context if their definitions and periods match. Avoid creating an unsupported overall star rating by combining unrelated ratios. Use each measure for the question it actually answers, then compare the product documents.'],
    ['A practical way to compare two reports',
      'Create columns for insurer, business segment, financial year, count-based paid measure, amount-based measure if provided, pending cases and complaints. Copy the definitions and note any missing metric. Mark a measure unavailable when the table does not provide it instead of inferring it from another ratio.',
      'Review the current regulator or insurer report rather than a search snippet labelled with the current calendar year. Publishing an older financial-year figure can be useful when its period is clearly stated; relabelling it as this year’s performance changes its meaning. Keep the reporting date visible in any comparison you share.']
  ],
  'insurance/term-insurance-real-value': [
    ['Estimate the obligation your family would need to replace',
      'Start with debts that would remain, essential household spending, dependent-care needs and time-specific goals. Subtract assets that survivors could actually use and existing suitable cover. Do not count an illiquid asset as immediately available cash or subtract savings already reserved for another essential purpose.',
      'For a hypothetical household, ₹30 lakh debt plus ₹60 lakh planned income-replacement provision minus ₹10 lakh accessible savings suggests an ₹80 lakh gap before other goals. This is a planning illustration, not a recommended sum assured. The horizon, inflation, survivors’ income and investment assumptions can materially change it.'],
    ['Choose the term and premium structure together',
      'The policy term is the period of protection; the premium-payment term is how long premiums are due. A limited-pay structure can concentrate payments early without extending cover forever. Compare total scheduled premiums, payment timing and the effect on your budget, not only the first annual premium.',
      'Select the horizon around the actual dependency and debt timeline rather than automatically choosing the maximum age offered. Keep provision for renewals and payment continuity. An affordable policy that remains in force serves a different purpose from a larger policy that is allowed to lapse.'],
    ['Return of premium is a separate financial trade-off',
      'If a plan returns specified premiums on survival, read what is returned and which taxes, rider costs or extra charges are excluded. Compare its extra premium with a plain term option for the same cover and term. Money returned decades later is not equivalent to money available today.',
      'Avoid valuing a sum assured as an investment return. Term protection is designed to address a specified insured event. Riders such as critical illness or accidental death can have different definitions and payout conditions; adding their printed limits together does not describe one unconditional benefit.'],
    ['Make the proposal and family records usable',
      'Disclose health, occupation, habits and existing insurance as the proposal requires. Keep the submitted answers, medical reports, issued schedule and acceptance terms. An intermediary’s verbal reassurance is not a replacement for accurate application information.',
      'Tell the nominee where to find the policy number, insurer contact, premium records and claim procedure. Review the nomination and family obligations after major changes such as marriage, children or a new debt. For a replacement policy, secure issuance and terms before giving up existing protection.']
  ],
  'insurance/room-rent-trap': [
    ['Translate the room clause into a hospital choice',
      'A room entitlement can be a named category, a rupee cap or a percentage of cover. Check which definition the policy uses and whether the hospital’s terminology matches it. “Single private room” and a particular deluxe category may not be interchangeable. ICU conditions may be listed separately.',
      'If a hypothetical policy limits room rent to ₹5,000 a day and the chosen room costs ₹8,000, the ₹3,000 daily difference is a starting point, not the complete claim calculation. The wording may also address associated expenses, while specific categories may have separate treatment. Obtain the clause before assuming only the room difference is payable by you.'],
    ['Proportional deduction requires the actual eligible base',
      'Under an illustrative clause that scales specified linked charges by the eligible-room ratio, ₹5,000 divided by ₹8,000 gives 62.5%. A ₹1 lakh linked eligible amount would become ₹62,500 under that assumed clause. It would be incorrect to apply the ratio to every bill entry without checking what the actual policy includes.',
      'Ask the insurer for an itemised explanation distinguishing room costs, charges subject to a room-linked adjustment and charges outside it. Then account for any other limits or cost sharing separately. Generic charts can explain the concept but cannot replace the policy-specific bill assessment.'],
    ['Plan for unavailability and emergency admission',
      'For a planned admission, request a room within entitlement and retain the hospital estimate. If that room is unavailable, document the allocation and ask the insurer how the policy handles it. Do not assume an automatic exemption merely because the hospital assigned the room.',
      'In an emergency, medical care comes first. Keep the subsequent admission and room records and contact the insurer or claim administrator promptly through the policy’s process. A later dispute is easier to examine with dates, available room categories and written authorisations.'],
    ['Read beyond a no-cap headline',
      'A policy without a stated monetary room cap can still contain a room-category entitlement, treatment limits, exclusions, deductible or co-payment. Compare the entire claim scenario. A no-cap feature should solve a specific accommodation concern, not become a shorthand for full reimbursement.',
      'Use the same hospital and treatment scenario when comparing policies. Include the premium difference and the likely out-of-pocket risks for your household. A room upgrade you would never choose should not dominate the buying decision, while an unaffordable entitled room category needs attention.']
  ],
  'insurance/health-insurance-for-parents': [
    ['Compare the conditions most relevant to the parents',
      'List existing diagnoses, medicines, recent procedures and hospitals used. Review the proposal questions and disclose the required history accurately. Ask how the insurer’s issued terms treat the relevant conditions, waiting periods and exclusions rather than relying on an age-based premium table alone.',
      'A medical examination does not replace disclosure of information the proposal asks for. Retain the submitted proposal and reports. A policy accepted with an exclusion or co-payment is different from the cover originally illustrated by an agent; read the issued schedule before treating the application as complete.'],
    ['Separate policies and a family floater have different exposure',
      'Two people sharing a floater draw on the same available pool. Separate cover can keep one person’s claim from using the other person’s base amount, but the prices and features may differ. Test a year with admissions for both parents rather than assuming only one claim.',
      'Check any restoration benefit for its actual trigger and use conditions. It may not apply to the first claim or to every repeat illness. Compare available cover after a claim, room entitlement and cost sharing alongside the annual premium.'],
    ['Budget for recurring premium and out-of-pocket costs',
      'In a hypothetical case, a 20% co-payment on ₹4 lakh of otherwise admissible expenses leaves ₹80,000 for the insured, before other non-covered amounts. A premium discount can be small relative to that exposure. Calculate the reserve your family would need rather than choosing the lowest premium automatically.',
      'Consider whether future premium payments remain affordable if income changes. Do not present an assumed annual premium increase as a guaranteed schedule. Ask the insurer which factors can change renewal pricing and check the current product terms.'],
    ['Make the claim process accessible to the family',
      'Confirm network hospitals near the parents and how cashless requests are initiated. Keep policy details, official support contacts, identity documents and medical records accessible to the person likely to help during admission. Explain who can coordinate paperwork and what costs may require payment.',
      'When replacing or porting cover, review acceptance and continuity before allowing the old policy to end. A new application is not an issued replacement. Record any waiting-period credit and additional-cover conditions in the new documents.']
  ],
  'insurance/copay-vs-no-copay': [
    ['Calculate the share on the admissible amount',
      'Co-payment describes the share of a covered claim that the insured bears under the policy. It does not make an excluded expense covered. In a hypothetical ₹2 lakh hospital bill with ₹20,000 excluded, a 10% co-payment applied to the remaining ₹1.8 lakh is ₹18,000. Total out-of-pocket spending would be ₹38,000 if no other deductions apply.',
      'Read whether the co-payment is mandatory, chosen, triggered by age or linked to a location or hospital arrangement. A contract can contain more than one condition. Ask how they interact instead of adding percentages without knowing the calculation base.'],
    ['Deductibles and co-payment are not interchangeable',
      'A deductible is a threshold defined by the policy; a co-payment is a share. A top-up deductible may be assessed per claim or over an aggregate period depending on the product. That difference affects whether several smaller admissions cross it.',
      'If both a deductible and co-payment apply, their order changes the result. For a purely hypothetical ₹2 lakh admissible claim, a ₹50,000 deductible followed by 20% co-payment on ₹1.5 lakh leaves an insurer amount of ₹1.2 lakh. This uses the stated sequence only; it is not a universal health-policy formula.'],
    ['Compare the premium saving with realistic claim exposure',
      'Suppose selecting co-payment saves ₹4,000 in annual premium and means a 20% share of an otherwise admissible claim. ₹20,000 of admissible expenses produces ₹4,000 co-payment under those simplified assumptions. That arithmetic is a one-period comparison, not a forecast of claim frequency or lifetime value.',
      'Test a low-claim and a high-claim year, and consider whether you can fund the larger out-of-pocket amount. A lower premium can be reasonable for some budgets, but the decision should explicitly accept that risk. Do not choose a percentage you could not afford during a significant admission.'],
    ['No co-payment does not mean no deductions',
      'Room conditions, waiting periods, exclusions and sub-limits can still apply when the co-payment is zero. Compare the full policy for the same treatment and hospital. Otherwise a no-copay label can distract from a more significant restriction.',
      'Keep the Customer Information Sheet and wording together and confirm any chosen option in the issued schedule. At claim time, request the itemised amount admitted and each deduction. This separates a dispute about coverage from a correctly applied contractual share.']
  ],
  'tax/old-vs-new-regime': [
    ['Rebuild the comparison from taxable salary',
      'Start with salary income for the period, not the employer’s total cost-to-company figure. CTC can include employer contributions and benefits whose treatment differs from cash salary. Reconcile the salary statement and Form 16 with the income actually included in the computation. Separate income from interest, rent, capital gains or business before using a salary-only model.',
      'For an AY 2026–27 illustration, a resident individual below 60 with ₹15 lakh ordinary salary, no other income and only the standard deduction has ₹14.5 lakh taxable salary under the old regime and ₹14.25 lakh under the new regime. Applying the published slabs and 4% cess gives ₹2,57,400 under the old regime and ₹97,500 under the new regime. This limited example does not include surcharge, special-rate income or additional eligible claims.',
      'Changing the old-regime assumptions changes the comparison. If the same person has an additional ₹4 lakh of genuinely eligible old-regime exemptions and deductions, old taxable income becomes ₹10.5 lakh and tax including cess becomes ₹1,32,600. If those additional eligible amounts instead total ₹6 lakh, taxable income becomes ₹8.5 lakh and tax including cess becomes ₹85,800. The examples show why neither a universal winner nor one deduction threshold works for every salary. The amounts are inputs, not a claim that every taxpayer can deduct ₹4 lakh or ₹6 lakh.'],
    ['A slab, a rebate and marginal relief do different jobs',
      'Slabs calculate tax on successive parts of taxable income. The highest applicable percentage does not apply to every rupee. A rebate then reduces eligible calculated tax under its conditions. Cess is applied to the remaining applicable tax. Keep these steps separate when checking a calculator or a worked example.',
      'For AY 2026–27, the new-regime rebate for qualifying resident individuals can eliminate tax on ordinary total income up to ₹12 lakh. With only eligible salary and a ₹75,000 standard deduction, that corresponds to ₹12.75 lakh gross salary in this restricted illustration. It is not a general exemption for ₹12.75 lakh of any kind of income. Special-rate income and rebate eligibility require their own treatment.',
      'Above the ₹12 lakh ordinary-income threshold, marginal relief can limit the immediate tax increase under the applicable conditions. Do not subtract the maximum rebate from every higher income or assume tax suddenly applies to gross salary at one flat rate. Use the computation for the selected period and include any special-rate income before drawing a final conclusion.'],
    ['Count deductions you already qualify for before buying anything',
      'Inventory eligible provident-fund contributions, insurance premiums, tuition payments, rent-related exemption, loan-interest claims and other applicable amounts. Check the relevant limits and conditions separately. A total payment to a product is not necessarily the allowed deduction, and the same payment cannot be counted twice.',
      'For example, if a combined deduction limit is already filled by existing eligible payments, buying another product will not increase the deduction under that limit. Keep additional personal NPS contribution and employer NPS contribution separate: they have different provisions and conditions. The site’s salary calculator does not model every employer contribution or complex return adjustment.',
      'A tax saving is also not the entire invested amount. An illustrative ₹1 lakh deduction at a 30% marginal rate with 4% cess saves ₹31,200 if the full deduction actually reduces income taxed at that rate and no other interaction changes the result. The remaining money is still committed to the expense or investment. Evaluate lock-in, liquidity and risk before chasing a deduction.'],
    ['Payroll selection and return filing are separate steps',
      'Tell payroll which regime and documented claims should be used for withholding, then review the eventual return using the applicable election rules. Tax deducted from salary is a credit against final tax, not proof that the selected computation is correct. A refund or additional payment can arise when other income or final eligible claims differ from payroll estimates.',
      'Taxpayers with business or professional income can face different election procedures and restrictions from salary-only taxpayers. Do not copy advice about changing regimes annually without identifying the income type. Check the relevant form, deadline and year before making the election.',
      'The 2026 legal transition makes the period especially important. Income for FY 2025–26 still belongs to AY 2026–27 under the 1961 Act; income from April 2026 uses the new tax-year terminology and renumbered provisions. The figures above are explicitly AY 2026–27 examples. The current-period selector in the calculator should be used for a different period rather than silently reusing old section references.'],
    ['Review the final comparison before filing',
      'Use the same complete income base for both available options. Reconcile interest certificates, capital-gain records, withholding and eligible exemptions. Check age and residential status where they affect the calculation. A salary example for someone below 60 cannot be transferred unchanged to a pensioner whose old-regime basic threshold differs.',
      'Keep a short worksheet recording gross income, each allowed adjustment, taxable income, slab tax, rebate or relief, surcharge where applicable and cess. Compare the final liability and retain the supporting evidence. This makes a difference between payroll and the return explainable instead of treating the lower number as automatically correct.']
  ],
  'tax/80c-beyond-the-obvious': [
    ['Start with payments already made during the year',
      'For a period and regime where the deduction is available, list eligible employee provident-fund contributions, qualifying life-insurance premium, tuition fees, housing-loan principal and other eligible payments already made. Each has conditions. Do not assume every insurance premium, education bill or property payment qualifies simply because it resembles a category in a tax-saving advertisement.',
      'Create a ledger with payer, beneficiary, date, amount, applicable provision and supporting receipt. Reconcile the employee contribution separately from an employer contribution. The combined limit is applied after eligibility checks; it is not a separate allowance for each product.'],
    ['Understand the saving before filling the remaining limit',
      'If ₹1.2 lakh of eligible payments already fills a hypothetical ₹1.5 lakh combined limit, only ₹30,000 remains under that limit. A further ₹70,000 investment does not produce a ₹70,000 deduction there. An unused limit is an option to evaluate, not an obligation to buy a product.',
      'The value of a deduction depends on the tax computation. It reduces eligible taxable income rather than refunding the investment. A taxpayer with no tax remaining after an applicable rebate may receive no additional immediate saving from another deduction. Compare the final liability before committing cash.'],
    ['Match the product to the goal and lock-in',
      'A deposit, provident-fund account, equity-linked fund and insurance contract have different risks, access rules and purposes. A tax-saving label does not turn market returns into a guarantee or make a long-duration insurance commitment equivalent to a short savings account.',
      'Set aside emergency and near-term spending money before selecting a locked product. Compare ongoing charges, surrender or premature-closure rules, investment risk and the amount needed in later years. A hurried March purchase can create a multi-year obligation for a one-year deduction.'],
    ['Keep separate provisions separate',
      'An additional contribution deduction or a health-insurance deduction does not automatically enlarge the combined savings-payment cap. Read the relevant provision, qualifying person and limits. Personal NPS and employer NPS require different treatment, and one contribution should not appear under two deductions.',
      'For income from April 2026, use the corresponding provisions of the 2025 Act, including section 123 and Schedule XV where applicable, rather than transferring the old section label without checking the regime. Retain the payment certificates and the calculation used in the final return.']
  ],
  'tax/hra-calculation': [
    ['Use the salary definition and the relevant period',
      'For the AY 2026–27 old-regime calculation, the relevant salary definition is not total CTC. Basic salary, qualifying dearness allowance and turnover-based commission have the specified treatment. Match the salary, HRA received, rent paid and location for the period being calculated. A change in employer, salary, rent or city can require separate period calculations.',
      'The exemption is the least of actual HRA received, rent paid minus 10% of relevant salary, and 50% of relevant salary for the specified metro cities or 40% elsewhere, subject to eligibility. The specified cities are Mumbai, Kolkata, Delhi and Chennai; everyday use of the word “metro” does not automatically place another city in that tax category. These are AY 2026–27 rules, not an assumption about every regime or period.'],
    ['Work through one example instead of deducting the whole rent',
      'Assume an eligible old-regime AY 2026–27 case with ₹6 lakh relevant annual salary, ₹2.4 lakh HRA received and ₹2.16 lakh actual rent paid for the same period outside the specified metros. The three amounts are ₹2.4 lakh, ₹1.56 lakh and ₹2.4 lakh. The least is ₹1.56 lakh. The remaining ₹84,000 of received HRA is not exempt under that calculation.',
      'This example assumes the stated amounts meet all eligibility conditions. If rent changes halfway through the year, do not substitute a full year of the later rent. Calculate the appropriate periods and keep payment evidence. A negative rent-minus-salary component does not create a negative exemption.'],
    ['Documentation should support a genuine rental arrangement',
      'Keep the agreement, receipts or payment trail, landlord details and the evidence requested by payroll and the applicable filing rules. Payments to a related person need the same underlying reality of tenancy and payment; a receipt alone does not turn living rent-free into an eligible rental expense.',
      'Payroll may request documents and apply withholding based on submitted claims. The return still needs a correct claim for the actual period and regime. Reconcile the salary certificate and reported exemption rather than assuming every amount payroll accepted is automatically final.'],
    ['Home ownership and HRA require separate eligibility checks',
      'Owning a property does not by itself answer whether rent paid elsewhere qualifies for HRA exemption. Nor does receiving HRA establish eligibility for a home-loan-interest deduction. Residence, property use, payments and the applicable provisions must be considered separately.',
      'If you do not receive HRA, do not use the HRA formula as a substitute for any other rent-related deduction. That other provision has its own conditions and filing requirements. For the new regime or the new Act’s tax year, confirm which exemption is available before carrying this old-regime example into a calculation.']
  ],
  'tax/tax-on-credit-card-rewards': [
    ['Identify what the benefit actually represents',
      'A reduction in the purchase price, cashback associated with a purchase, a reward point, a contest prize and a referral payment do not necessarily have the same factual character. Record why the benefit was received, who paid it and whether it relates to a personal purchase or a business activity. The app’s label alone does not settle tax treatment.',
      'Keep the purchase invoice and benefit record together. A cash reward visible in an account is different from an unredeemed point balance with restrictions. Do not announce that every reward is tax-free or that every point must be treated as cash income at an assumed conversion rate.'],
    ['Business spending needs consistent accounts',
      'Where a purchase is recorded as a business expense, examine how a linked discount or cashback is treated in the books and the relevant tax rules. An invoice recorded at full cost and a later related benefit should be reconciled, rather than ignored because it arrived through a credit-card programme.',
      'Separate personal and business transactions, retain statements and document who owns the reward under employer or company policy. Rewards earned on reimbursed expenses may also raise accounting or policy questions beyond the individual cardholder’s preferences. Seek a qualified review for material amounts.'],
    ['Referral and promotional payments deserve separate attention',
      'A benefit for introducing a customer or performing a promotional activity is a different event from a price reduction on goods you bought. Record any campaign terms, payment certificate and withholding information. An issuer describing the payment as a reward does not eliminate the need to identify its nature.',
      'Similarly, a competition prize or business-linked incentive should not be folded into a blanket statement about ordinary retail cashback. Match the facts to the applicable tax period and provision. A threshold from an unrelated gift rule is not proof that all benefits below it can be ignored.'],
    ['Use records to resolve uncertainty',
      'Create a ledger for significant benefits showing the associated transaction, nominal amount, redemption, date and intended accounting treatment. Reconcile it to statements and any tax information made available by the payer. Avoid counting a hypothetical catalogue valuation as money received.',
      'If the treatment is material or unclear, take the actual terms and records to a tax professional. A useful question specifies the activity, payer, personal or business context and whether the benefit was cash or restricted redemption. That produces a more meaningful answer than asking whether credit-card rewards in general are taxable.']
  ],
  'savings/best-fd-rates-2026': [
    ['Compare the same maturity date and depositor category',
      'Start with the date you need the money, then compare deposits that mature near it. A special 400-day offer and a two-year ordinary deposit solve different timing needs. Record whether the quoted rate applies to your amount, age, resident status, booking channel and callable or non-callable product. A rate available to one customer category should not be shown as the standard rate for everyone.',
      'A cumulative deposit retains interest according to its terms; a payout deposit supplies periodic income. Match the payout frequency when comparing. An advertised annual rate is not necessarily the effective annual yield after compounding, tax and payout conventions. Use the issuer’s maturity amount for the exact product and verify whether any discounted monthly-payout method applies.'],
    ['A small rate premium has a measurable rupee value',
      'For a simple hypothetical one-year ₹5 lakh deposit, a 7% rate produces ₹35,000 interest before tax while 7.5% produces ₹37,500. The difference is ₹2,500. If the incremental interest is taxed at an assumed 30% plus 4% cess, the after-tax difference is about ₹1,720. These simple-interest assumptions are not live offers or a statement of every depositor’s tax rate.',
      'This helps frame the decision. A higher rate may be useful, but its rupee benefit should be weighed against product access, early-closure conditions, issuer type and the effort of managing another account. Do not treat a half-point premium as a reason to disregard the nature of the deposit or its protection.'],
    ['Check early closure using the period actually held',
      'An issuer may recalculate interest using the rate applicable to the time actually completed and then apply its stated penalty. That is different from simply subtracting a penalty from the originally booked rate. Non-callable or specially restricted deposits may not provide normal voluntary early withdrawal at all.',
      'As a hypothetical example, a two-year 7.5% booking closed after one year might use an applicable one-year rate of 6.5% less a 1 percentage-point penalty, leaving 5.5% for that illustration. The actual rate table, calculation basis and exceptions come from the issuer. Request a worked early-closure amount for the point at which you might need funds.'],
    ['Leave headroom for interest in deposit-insurance planning',
      'For eligible insured-bank deposits, the DICGC limit includes interest as well as principal and aggregates deposits in the same right and capacity at the same bank. A ₹5 lakh principal balance does not leave headroom for accruing interest under that limit. Multiple branches or multiple deposit receipts at one bank do not automatically create separate limits.',
      'Check the legal bank name rather than relying on different account brands. A company or NBFC deposit is a different product from an insured-bank deposit; a credit rating does not make it DICGC-insured. Deposit insurance also has a claims process and is not a promise of instantaneous access to cash in every disruption. Keep emergency-access planning separate from the insurance limit.'],
    ['Build a deposit ladder around actual expenses',
      'If money is needed in stages, consider separate maturities instead of locking everything for the longest high-rate tenure. For a hypothetical ₹3 lakh goal with ₹1 lakh needed in each of three successive years, matching deposits to those dates can reduce the need to break one long deposit. The rate available when later deposits renew remains uncertain.',
      'Track deposit number, maturity instructions, nominee, payout account and renewal setting. Auto-renewal can move money into a new term when you intended to use it. Review the bank’s rate and instructions before maturity and retain the final advice. Good administration can matter more than a small advertised premium that is lost through an avoidable early closure.']
  ],
  'savings/fd-real-return': [
    ['Separate quoted rate, credited interest and spendable interest',
      'The stated deposit rate describes a product before your personal tax and inflation. The credited amount follows the issuer’s day-count, compounding and payout terms. Spendable interest is what remains after the applicable tax, which may differ from tax withheld by the bank. Comparing an FD with another investment requires the same after-tax basis.',
      'For a hypothetical one-year ₹1 lakh deposit earning ₹7,000 before tax, an assumed 20% tax plus 4% cess on that tax leaves ₹5,544 interest. TDS, if deducted, is an advance credit against the final liability rather than a second permanent tax to subtract. Avoid calling the withheld percentage the investor’s true tax rate.'],
    ['Use the real-return formula consistently',
      'Real return adjusts purchasing power: (1 + nominal after-tax return) divided by (1 + inflation), minus one. With a hypothetical 5.544% after-tax return and 5% inflation, that gives approximately 0.52%, not the full 7% headline rate. Inflation and personal tax here are assumptions, not current measured values or a forecast.',
      'For small rates, subtracting inflation gives an approximation, but use the ratio when presenting a precise example. A negative real return means reduced purchasing power under the assumptions; it does not mean the bank has deducted inflation from the account balance.'],
    ['Liquidity and reinvestment change the experience',
      'A deposit held to maturity and a deposit closed early can earn different amounts. Include the recalculated interest and any penalty when assessing an actual return. An overdraft against an FD has its own borrowing cost and does not make access to the deposit free.',
      'When a deposit matures, the next available rate may be higher or lower. A booked fixed rate for one term does not guarantee the same income over a decade of renewals. Align maturities with planned uses and keep a separate reserve for immediate expenses.'],
    ['Choose stability for the purpose it serves',
      'A nominally stable deposit can be useful for a dated obligation even if its real return is modest. A market-linked alternative may have greater return potential but can lose value before that date. Compare risk, access and timing alongside expected return.',
      'Review the issuer, deposit protection, ownership capacity and total balance including interest. Keep the renewal instructions and interest records accessible. A deposit should be judged against the job you need it to do rather than a promised investment ranking based on one illustrative rate.']
  ],
  'savings/ppf-vs-fd-vs-debt-fund': [
    ['The three products solve different timing needs',
      'A PPF account has a long scheme-defined maturity with conditional access provisions. An FD has a selected tenure and product-specific closure conditions. A debt mutual fund holds securities whose value can move and whose redemption process follows the scheme. Treating all three as interchangeable safe savings overlooks the differences in access and risk.',
      'Start with your goal date and whether capital may need to be withdrawn unexpectedly. Then compare the available product terms. A higher illustrative return is not useful if the money cannot be accessed when the obligation falls due.'],
    ['Compare the cash-flow pattern before the yield',
      'Periodic contributions to PPF, a single FD investment and monthly mutual-fund purchases involve different investment dates. A ten-year ending balance is not comparable unless the amount and timing invested are the same. Separate contributions from investment growth.',
      'An FD booked at a fixed rate for one tenure does not guarantee that rate on subsequent renewals. PPF rates can change through the official notification process; do not freeze the latest rate for an entire long-term projection without labelling that assumption. A fund return is market-dependent and should not be presented as a contractual coupon.'],
    ['Read the risks inside a debt fund',
      'Debt funds can be exposed to interest-rate changes, credit events and liquidity conditions. A portfolio of government securities can still change in market value as yields move. A corporate-debt portfolio raises additional issuer-credit questions. The fund’s category, duration, holdings and Riskometer matter.',
      'There is no DICGC insurance on mutual-fund units. Check the scheme information document, expenses, exit load and redemption timeline. A fund’s historical return during falling rates is not evidence that the next year will produce the same gain.'],
    ['Compare tax and withdrawal terms separately',
      'Product taxation can depend on the acquisition date, fund classification, tax year and investor circumstances. Verify those rules before using an old tax-efficiency chart. A contribution deduction and the tax treatment of earnings or redemption are separate questions.',
      'Use current official PPF rules for maturity and permitted access, the bank’s FD documents for early closure, and the fund documents for redemption. A suitable combination may allocate money by goal rather than forcing every rupee into the product with the highest projected percentage.']
  ],
  'savings/senior-citizen-fd': [
    ['Match deposit income to the household budget',
      'A senior-citizen rate premium can increase interest, but check eligibility, deposit type and payout frequency. Do not assume every bank or scheme offers the same additional rate. The quote may also depend on tenure, amount and whether it is a special scheme.',
      'At a hypothetical 7.5% simple annual rate, ₹20 lakh produces ₹1.5 lakh annual interest before tax, equivalent to ₹12,500 per month on average. That average is not a guarantee of the actual monthly payout, which follows the product’s convention. Compare the credited cash with recurring expenses and keep a reserve for irregular medical or repair costs.'],
    ['Cumulative and payout deposits serve different needs',
      'A cumulative deposit builds an amount for maturity while a payout deposit helps fund current expenses. Choosing cumulative interest and then repeatedly breaking deposits for bills can defeat the purpose. Schedule maturities or payout options around actual cash needs.',
      'Review renewal risk: a new term can have a lower rate, affecting future income. Test a budget using a lower assumed renewal rate before relying on today’s quoted amount indefinitely. Inflation can also change how far a fixed nominal payout goes.'],
    ['Do not confuse withholding relief with exemption',
      'A form requesting eligible non-deduction of tax is subject to its conditions; age alone does not establish eligibility. TDS and final tax liability are different. Check total income, the relevant year’s rules and all interest received before submitting declarations.',
      'Use any senior-citizen interest deduction only where the applicable regime and provision allow it. Keep interest certificates, withholding records and deposit ownership details together. Do not assume a familiar historical section number applies unchanged to income under the new Act.'],
    ['Check ownership, insurance and administration',
      'Record the principal holder, joint holding, capacity, nominee and operating instructions. Deposit-insurance aggregation follows the legal bank and right and capacity, not simply the number of receipts. The limit includes accrued interest.',
      'Review access arrangements for the person who may need to assist with banking and claims. Compare a bank FD with a senior-citizen savings scheme using the latter’s current eligibility, limits, payout and closure rules, not only a headline rate. Keep nomination and maturity instructions up to date.']
  ],
  'savings/tax-saving-fd-vs-elss-vs-ppf': [
    ['A shared deduction does not make products equivalent',
      'Where the relevant tax period and regime permit a deduction, the benefit belongs to an eligible payment under the applicable limit. It does not guarantee the investment return. Check how much eligible deduction remains after existing contributions and payments before comparing new products.',
      'A tax-saving bank deposit provides product-defined nominal interest, ELSS holds market-linked equity and PPF follows its government scheme. Their liquidity, risk and time horizons differ. Choose for the goal and ability to bear loss, then account for any permitted tax benefit.'],
    ['Lock-in follows the actual account or purchase',
      'A tax-saving FD normally has a five-year lock-in, while ELSS units generally have a three-year lock-in for each allotment. A monthly ELSS SIP therefore releases units on different dates rather than unlocking the entire account three years after the first contribution. Check the scheme and deposit terms for applicable conditions.',
      'PPF maturity is based on the scheme’s financial-year rules, not three or five years from each deposit. Conditional withdrawals or premature closure do not make it an ordinary emergency account. Keep money for immediate obligations outside products whose access terms do not fit those needs.'],
    ['Model the same money over the same period',
      'Compare an identical contribution schedule. Investing ₹1.5 lakh at the start of each year and ₹12,500 monthly do not put money to work for the same length of time. Label the dates and assumed returns in any projection.',
      'For a market-linked product, show that the projected value can differ materially and may be below contributions at a goal date. For a deposit, include the applicable tax on interest and any reinvestment after maturity. For PPF, a constant-rate projection is only an assumption if the official rate can change.'],
    ['Separate entry, earnings and exit taxation',
      'An allowed contribution deduction does not automatically make interest or sale proceeds tax-free. Each stage has its own treatment, and fund taxation can depend on the applicable rules. Use the law for the income period and actual investment rather than a blanket EEE or ETE label without conditions.',
      'Avoid buying a long commitment only to save this year’s tax. Check fees, contribution obligations and the consequences of early exit or lapse. If existing eligible payments already fill the available cap, assess the new investment on its own merits without attributing a second deduction to it.']
  ],
  'mutual-funds/cagr-vs-actual-return': [
    ['CAGR describes one starting value and one ending value',
      'For a single ₹1 lakh investment growing to ₹1.5 lakh over exactly three years without intervening cash flows, CAGR is (1.5) raised to one-third, minus one, or about 14.47% annually. It summarises those endpoints; it does not say that each year earned 14.47% or that the value never fell.',
      'A fund factsheet’s return over a selected window describes that measurement period and plan. Your purchase date, additional contributions and withdrawals can be different. A comparison must also match the scheme, direct or regular plan, growth or distribution option and date.'],
    ['Use cash-flow dates for your personal return',
      'For several purchases and withdrawals, record investments as negative cash flows, proceeds as positive flows and the current or final portfolio value as a positive closing flow on the valuation date. XIRR uses those actual dates to estimate an annualised money-weighted return.',
      'A SIP investor adding money shortly before a market decline can experience a different result from an investor who invested once at the start. That does not imply the factsheet calculation is necessarily wrong; it measures a different sequence. Do not calculate personal CAGR by treating every monthly contribution as if it had been invested on day one.'],
    ['Match benchmark and observation windows',
      'Compare performance over identical dates and against an appropriate benchmark, including the same return convention where applicable. A price-only index and an index including distributions are different comparisons. A short strong window should not be substituted for a longer period just because its annualised number is larger.',
      'Rolling-period analysis can show how returns varied across entry dates, but the window length, frequency and data set must be stated. Historical ranges do not create a guaranteed minimum future return. Avoid publishing a universal market-return range without a dated supporting calculation.'],
    ['Reconcile value, costs and tax',
      'Reported NAV-based performance reflects ongoing fund expenses in the NAV; subtracting the expense ratio again from the same return can double-count it. Your realised cash can still be affected by exit load and tax. Separate pre-tax portfolio performance from money available after selling.',
      'Use an account statement to reconcile units, NAV, purchases, redemptions and distributions. Check the sign and date of each cash flow if a spreadsheet return looks implausible. XIRR is a measurement of the recorded experience, not a prediction of next year’s result.']
  ],
  'mutual-funds/direct-vs-regular': [
    ['Compare plans of the same scheme and option',
      'AMFI explains that direct and regular plans of a scheme have the same underlying portfolio but different expense structures and separate NAVs. Direct plans exclude distributor commission, which contributes to a lower expense ratio. Comparing two unrelated schemes instead will mix plan costs with different investment decisions.',
      'A higher NAV is not, by itself, a reason that a fund is expensive to buy. Your money buys a corresponding number of units. Compare percentage performance, expenses and the same dates rather than choosing the smaller NAV as if it were a discounted share price.'],
    ['Costs compound over the period you stay invested',
      'In a purely hypothetical projection, ₹1 lakh compounded for ten years at 10% becomes about ₹2,59,374, while at 9% it becomes about ₹2,36,736. The ₹22,638 difference illustrates compounding of a one-point net-return gap. It does not promise those rates or an actual expense difference for a named fund.',
      'Actual expenses and market returns can change. Review the scheme’s published expense ratios and your holding period rather than projecting the current gap unchanged for decades. Expenses are already reflected in the plan’s NAV-based return, so do not subtract them twice.'],
    ['Distribution and advice are different arrangements',
      'A regular-plan distributor may provide assistance, but clarify the service and compensation. A fee-based adviser can recommend direct plans under a different arrangement. Direct investing removes distributor commission from that plan; it does not automatically supply personalised portfolio advice.',
      'Assess your ability to select an appropriate allocation, review risk and avoid impulsive changes. Choosing a lower-cost plan and then repeatedly switching on performance headlines can still produce poor outcomes. Compare the overall cost of any advice or service you actually use.'],
    ['Switching can create a transaction, not just a setting change',
      'A switch between plans is generally implemented as redemption and purchase, which can have tax and exit-load implications. Check the actual scheme, holding period and applicable rules before moving an existing investment. Do not assume changing a platform preference retroactively changes all held units.',
      'Confirm the plan and option on new purchases and mandates. Keep records of any switch and the new allotments. Consider future contributions separately from selling old units, and evaluate the one-time cost against the likely future expense saving over your expected holding period.']
  ],
  'mutual-funds/sip-advertised-vs-actual': [
    ['A SIP is a contribution method, not a return guarantee',
      'Regular investing schedules purchases across dates. It does not change the underlying fund’s assets or eliminate market risk. In a falling market a contribution can buy more units, but the total holding can still be worth less than the money invested.',
      'A chart using a constant annual return is a projection under an assumption, not evidence that monthly returns are smooth. Read whether the calculation puts contributions at the beginning or end of each month, how it converts the annual rate and whether it includes a step-up.'],
    ['Contribution timing changes the result',
      'Two investors contributing the same total can end with different values when their purchase dates differ. A missed instalment, larger late contribution or withdrawal changes the cash-flow sequence. A fund’s point-to-point return therefore does not describe every SIP investor’s experience.',
      'Use actual allotment dates and amounts to compute a money-weighted return such as XIRR. A debit instruction date and the applicable NAV date may differ under the transaction rules. Reconcile the account statement rather than assuming every scheduled debit purchased units at a particular day’s NAV.'],
    ['Test a projection against the goal date',
      'A goal needs a required amount, contribution capacity and date. Show a lower-return scenario as well as the chosen assumption, and consider a period of decline near the goal. Do not treat extending the term as a free solution when the expense has a fixed deadline.',
      'Separate projected growth from contributions. If increasing monthly contributions is the main reason the target becomes attainable, say so. An annual step-up also requires future income to support the larger amount; it should not appear as automatic investment performance.'],
    ['Costs, taxes and discipline belong in the plan',
      'Review fund category, asset allocation, expense structure and any exit load. Each SIP purchase can have its own holding period for tax or lock-in purposes. A long-running SIP account does not make the most recent instalment an old investment.',
      'Keep emergency liquidity separate so a temporary cash need does not force a poorly timed sale. Review whether the fund still serves the goal rather than changing it for every short-term ranking. Regular contributions can support discipline, but the choice of assets and the ability to remain invested still matter.']
  ],
  'blog/cap-adjusted-cashback-explained': [
    ['Build a transaction worksheet before applying the percentage',
      'Use columns for date, posted date, merchant, amount, payment route, eligible reward bucket and expected reward. Keep excluded spending in the worksheet so the final household rate includes money that earned nothing. Do not allocate a transaction to two reward buckets unless the programme explicitly pays both benefits.',
      'For a hypothetical card with 5% on eligible online spending capped at ₹500 per month and 1% on eligible offline purchases, ₹12,000 online spending produces ₹500 rather than ₹600. ₹8,000 eligible offline spending produces ₹80 if that bucket has no cap reached. Add ₹5,000 excluded spending and the total is ₹580 over ₹25,000, or 2.32% before fees. Applying 5% to the entire budget would have predicted ₹1,250—more than twice the illustrated result.'],
    ['Compare reward caps with spending thresholds',
      'A reward cap limits earned points or cashback; a spend threshold decides when a benefit becomes available. A ₹500 reward cap at 5% is reached by ₹10,000 eligible spending. A milestone requiring ₹10,000 spending may instead give a separate benefit with additional conditions. Write down whether a printed amount measures purchases or rewards.',
      'Shared caps must be applied to the combined reward amount across their eligible buckets. Different percentages can consume the same cap at different spending levels. Identify whether overflow earns base rewards or none, and use the proper reset period. Do not multiply one capped month by twelve if your annual purchases are concentrated in two or three months.'],
    ['Calculate annual value from the actual spending pattern',
      'Under an illustrative ₹500 monthly cashback ceiling with no carry-forward, twelve months reaching the ceiling produce ₹6,000. Four months reaching it and eight months earning ₹100 produce only ₹2,800. The same annual total spending can therefore earn different rewards depending on when qualifying purchases occur.',
      'Suppose the illustrated annual reward is ₹6,000 and membership costs ₹1,000 plus ₹180 tax. Net reward after that membership cost is ₹4,820, before other charges. Divide by the annual amount spent for the household effective rate. Use the fee actually applicable and distinguish joining-year benefits from renewal-year value.'],
    ['Compare the alternative, not only the standalone reward',
      'A second card earning ₹3,000 is not automatically adding ₹3,000 if the first card would have earned ₹2,000 on those purchases. Incremental reward is ₹1,000 before the second membership fee and other costs. This comparison helps determine whether the extra account is worthwhile.',
      'Use a redemption you can actually access. Deduct redemption charges once, avoid valuing unused vouchers at full face value and compare travel prices like for like. Our calculators cover declared inputs and supported models; a manual worksheet is necessary when a merchant-specific route, per-transaction rounding or programme condition is not represented.'],
    ['Reconcile estimates with posted rewards',
      'At the end of the earning period, compare expected and credited amounts. Investigate missing transactions, later settlement, refunds, cap allocation and category classification. Keep the source terms and effective date used in the estimate. A reward correction should be based on the qualifying clause and transaction evidence.',
      'Do not move normal spending into a new date or payment route solely because a spreadsheet appears to earn more. Check merchant prices, fees, purchase need and repayment timing first. A cashback calculation describes eligible benefits; it does not make additional consumption a saving.']
  ],
  'blog/cashback-rate-is-a-lie': [
    ['Three rates answer three different questions',
      'The headline rate is the programme’s rate for a specified eligible purchase. The realised reward rate compares earned value with spending actually made. The net rate subtracts costs such as membership and redemption. Label each rate clearly, because a 5% headline and a 2% household result can both follow the stated terms.',
      'As a hypothetical example, ₹10,000 qualifying purchases at 5% earn ₹500. If another ₹10,000 of purchases is excluded, the household earns ₹500 over ₹20,000, or 2.5% before fees. No cap is needed to create the difference; eligibility alone changes the answer.'],
    ['Why a shopping description can mislead the estimate',
      'A merchant’s product category and the issuer’s reward classification can differ. Insurance bought on a website is still not necessarily eligible online retail. A wallet load, bill aggregator or marketplace checkout may follow a different payment route from a direct merchant purchase.',
      'Separate offer eligibility from ordinary rewards and fee-waiver spending. An instant merchant discount may have its own network, variant, EMI and date conditions. Check the issuer’s classification and the current terms rather than expecting every item described as online to earn the same percentage.'],
    ['Put the membership fee in the annual comparison',
      'Suppose a paid card adds an illustrative one percentage point of redeemable reward over your current option and costs ₹1,180 including tax. With no cap reached or other cost difference, it needs ₹1.18 lakh eligible annual spending to recover the fee. At ₹60,000, the additional ₹600 reward leaves a ₹580 deficit.',
      'That simple break-even assumes all spending qualifies and the additional rate remains applicable. Apply caps, exclusions and actual redemption values before using it for a product. A fee waiver is helpful only when you meet the relevant conditions using purchases you would make anyway.'],
    ['Use a year of evidence instead of a best-case month',
      'Review credited rewards, redeemed value, expired points and fees over a complete year. A large welcome reward or festival purchase can distort a short sample. Record first-year and ongoing benefits separately.',
      'Compare with the payment method or lower-fee card you would otherwise use. Extra reward from a new account is the difference between the alternatives, not the new card’s total reward alone. Keep interest on unpaid balances separate; it can overwhelm the percentage being compared.']
  ],
  'blog/best-card-freelancers-2026': [
    ['Sort the freelancer’s budget by merchant and currency',
      'Software subscriptions, advertising, cloud services, equipment, travel and professional fees are different payment scenarios. An online card label does not establish how each merchant will be classified. For subscriptions, record the billing currency, merchant country, renewal date and whether the issued network and payment controls support the recurring instruction.',
      'For a hypothetical ₹10,000-equivalent overseas subscription, an assumed 3% conversion markup plus 18% tax on that markup adds ₹354. If the eligible reward is ₹100, those two components alone leave ₹254 more cost than the base converted purchase. This simplified example excludes exchange-rate differences and other fees; it shows why a reward percentage cannot be evaluated separately from international-payment costs.'],
    ['Match statements to invoices rather than using the statement as the invoice',
      'A card statement identifies a payment but often does not provide the itemised purchase, supplier information or business purpose needed in accounts. Save the actual invoice, attach the transaction reference and label personal use separately. Reconcile refunds and credit notes so an expense is not left overstated after a return.',
      'For recurring services, keep a register of amount, cancellation process and next renewal date. A card replacement may affect a merchant token differently from an ordinary saved card number. Verify the renewal and cancellation result in the service account as well as the card account.'],
    ['Build a slow-payment scenario before using credit for cash flow',
      'A client invoice due before the card payment date can still arrive late. Budget the bill using money already available rather than treating an expected client transfer as certain. Separate funds reserved for tax, essential expenses and the full card statement.',
      'If using an instalment or a borrowing facility, compare its total cost, fees and repayment schedule. A retail credit card may have issuer restrictions on transaction purposes; read the actual product terms rather than assuming a personal card is suitable for all commercial use. Rewards do not make revolving debt inexpensive.'],
    ['Evaluate a card for its repeatable role',
      'Compare a no-fee option, an international-spending option and a category-specific option against your actual invoices. For each, use eligible purchases, caps, currency charges, redemption route and membership cost. An account should solve a recurring need, not only look attractive in a one-off application offer.',
      'Self-employed application documents and eligibility are issuer-specific. Check the list for the product and submit accurate income and identity information. Neither an informal income band nor owning another premium card guarantees approval. Consider the account-management effort and expected renewal cost before adding several due dates.']
  ],
  'blog/upi-vs-credit-cards-india': [
    ['Distinguish the funding account from the app',
      'An app can support more than one payment route. Bank-account UPI draws from the linked bank account, while a supported credit-card-on-UPI purchase draws on the card account. A wallet balance is another funding source. Similar checkout screens do not make their charges, billing or dispute processes identical.',
      'Before paying, inspect the selected funding account and merchant acceptance. A supported RuPay card linked in an app does not establish that every QR or transfer type can use it. If credit is unavailable for a particular transaction, do not assume the app has silently used the same card.'],
    ['Measure the purchase cost after the reward',
      'For a hypothetical ₹5,000 purchase, an eligible 1% reward is ₹50. If the merchant adds a ₹75 card-related charge that does not apply to bank-account UPI, the card costs ₹25 more after that reward, before any other fees. If there is no surcharge, the calculation changes. Check the actual checkout amount rather than selecting a route by its reward headline.',
      'Include whether the transaction qualifies, whether the cap is already used and whether the reward is cash or a restricted point. A scratch-card promotion with unknown outcome should not be budgeted as guaranteed cashback. A dated merchant discount has separate conditions.'],
    ['Repayment timing is useful only with a funded plan',
      'Credit can shift the date cash leaves the bank account, but the statement still requires repayment. Reserve the bill amount as purchases occur. A temporary delay in cash outflow is not income, and a payment through UPI funded by a credit card still contributes to the card obligation.',
      'Bank-account UPI can make the cash impact easier to see immediately. The better routine depends on the household’s budget and ability to reconcile statements. Do not use rewards as a reason to keep spending after the repayment reserve has been exhausted.'],
    ['Keep evidence when a payment fails or is disputed',
      'Save the payment reference, recipient or merchant details, amount, time and acknowledgement. Check whether the transaction succeeded, remains pending or was reversed before retrying. The complaint should identify the actual payment route and account involved.',
      'Contact the official app, bank or card-issuer support channel appropriate to the route. A card chargeback, UPI complaint and merchant refund are separate processes with conditions. No route guarantees that every purchase dispute will result in an automatic refund.']
  ],
  'blog/build-3-card-stack-guide': [
    ['Give each account a job before adding it',
      'List eligible recurring spending and identify the card you would currently use for each merchant. A general card can be the fallback, a category card can serve a genuinely different merchant or route, and a travel or international card can address a specific cost. Three is an example structure, not a required number.',
      'Two cards offering the same category rate under similar caps can duplicate each other rather than add value. Conversely, a category card whose capped bucket is useful for only one month of the year may not justify an annual fee. Match the roles to the twelve-month spending pattern.'],
    ['Calculate the marginal value of the next card',
      'Suppose a hypothetical second card raises usable rewards from 1% to 3% on ₹60,000 eligible annual purchases. Its additional reward is ₹1,200. If it costs ₹1,180 including tax, the estimated advantage is only ₹20 before other differences. A third card is evaluated against the existing two-card result, not against earning nothing.',
      'Apply the candidate’s caps and your existing card’s return on the same transactions. Count a welcome reward separately from renewal-year value. Avoid valuing every benefit at the advertised maximum or assuming every fee will be waived.'],
    ['Write a routing rule that can actually be followed',
      'A useful rule names the merchant or qualifying route, the card and the relevant limit. “Use the highest headline rate” is insufficient when transaction eligibility differs. Note what to use after a shared cap is exhausted and how a refund changes the earning total.',
      'Our tools estimate supported scenarios from the inputs collected. They do not inspect every merchant identifier, payment channel or transaction-level rule in your statement. Use their disclosed assumptions and check a manual calculation for a large purchase outside those inputs.'],
    ['Keep the management cost visible',
      'Record due dates, statement dates, annual-fee anniversaries, autopay accounts and reward expiry. Reconcile payments and rewards on each account. A small incremental reward is less compelling if extra accounts make missed or partial payments more likely.',
      'Review the stack annually and after a programme change. Remove a role from the plan when its eligible merchant disappears, its fee rises or you stop using its redemption. Before changing or closing an account, handle outstanding balances, subscriptions and unused points according to the issuer’s terms.']
  ],
  'blog/how-reward-points-work-india': [
    ['Turn an earning rate into a usable rupee rate',
      'For an illustrative programme earning two points for every complete ₹100 purchase, a redemption worth ₹0.25 per point gives ₹0.50 per ₹100 before rounding and costs, or 0.5%. A different redemption at ₹0.50 per point doubles the value under those assumptions. “Two points” alone is not a 2% cash return.',
      'A multiplier must also be interpreted relative to the base earning. Ten times one point is ten points, not necessarily ten rupees. Check whether the stated total includes base points or adds bonus points, and whether a separate accelerated-points ceiling applies.'],
    ['Account for co-payments and comparable prices',
      'If a redemption uses 10,000 points plus ₹2,000 for a booking you could otherwise buy for ₹5,000 on equivalent terms, the points replace ₹3,000, giving ₹0.30 per point before further costs. Comparing against a ₹9,000 rack rate for a different booking would overstate the saving.',
      'Match dates, cancellation terms, baggage or room conditions and any fees. A partner transfer ratio describes converted units, not the value of the eventual award. Availability, taxes and account rules matter. Transfer only when the planned redemption and conditions make sense.'],
    ['Separate reward inventory from realised benefit',
      'An unused balance is a potential benefit with programme restrictions. Record points earned, expired, redeemed and adjusted for returns. Track an approaching expiry before committing to an unnecessary purchase merely to use the balance.',
      'A minimum redemption and processing fee can make small redemptions unattractive. For a hypothetical ₹500-equivalent reward with a ₹99 fee, only ₹401 remains before other costs. Consider accumulating to a useful redemption if the terms and expiry permit it, rather than valuing every posted point at its best catalogue rate.'],
    ['Compare points with cashback using one spending basket',
      'Apply each product’s eligible earning to the same actual purchases, then use the redemption you expect to choose. Subtract membership and redemption costs and account for rewards you are unlikely to use. A points card can win a planned travel use case while a simpler cashback card can suit a different household.',
      'When programme rules change, recalculate future earning and existing-balance redemption separately. Previously earned points and future transactions can have different treatment under the notice. Keep official effective dates and avoid describing an anticipated devaluation as an established change without an issuer notice.']
  ],
  'blog/annual-fee-when-worth-paying': [
    ['Compare with the account you would otherwise keep',
      'A paid card producing ₹8,000 usable annual benefits is not an ₹8,000 gain if a no-fee card would already provide ₹5,000 on the same purchases. The incremental benefit is ₹3,000 before the paid membership cost. Compare alternatives against a single realistic budget.',
      'In a hypothetical case with a ₹2,360 tax-inclusive fee, that ₹3,000 improvement leaves ₹640 before other costs. If half the improved value depends on a lounge or voucher you will not use, the result becomes negative. A spreadsheet should change when your travel and merchant plans change.'],
    ['A waiver should follow planned eligible spending',
      'Review the eligible-spend definition, anniversary or measurement period and refund treatment for the waiver. Rewards eligibility and waiver eligibility are separate. A large excluded purchase may fail to count even though it increases the statement balance.',
      'Do not buy ₹10,000 of unwanted goods to avoid a ₹1,000 fee. If spending already planned will legitimately cross the threshold, account for that benefit. If it will not, compare paying the membership with a lower-fee alternative instead of manufacturing a purchase.'],
    ['Welcome benefits can distort the renewal decision',
      'Keep joining offers and repeatable annual benefits in separate columns. A welcome voucher can reduce year-one cost without helping the second year. A benefit conditional on payment of the renewal fee may not also apply when the fee is waived.',
      'Review the written retention or product-change offer for your account. Record amount, qualifying action, expiry and any changed fee. A successful waiver for another customer does not establish an entitlement or a universal negotiation success rate.'],
    ['Prepare before changing the account',
      'Review outstanding transactions, EMI obligations, recurring subscriptions and points before requesting a change or closure. Ask how an upgrade or downgrade affects membership date, credit limit, rewards and supplementary cards. Retain the issuer’s confirmation.',
      'A renewal review should use the next year’s expected use and the alternative available now. Keep the service or design preference separate from calculated savings. A card can be worth a personal convenience cost without needing to portray unused benefits as cash return.']
  ],
  'blog/best-card-high-spenders-2026': [
    ['High spending does not make every premium card economical',
      'Separate retail purchases from categories excluded or treated differently, such as tax, rent, insurance or particular business payments. Use the exact product terms. A large annual total can still generate limited rewards when the accelerated tier is capped or the largest purchases are excluded.',
      'Map purchases by earning period and merchant route. A single large purchase can hit a monthly cap that ordinary recurring spending would not. Annual fee-waiver eligibility, reward ceilings and redemption ceilings are different limits; record each separately.'],
    ['Choose the redemption route before estimating value',
      'A high point balance is useful only if the holder can redeem it effectively. Compare statement credit, catalogue use and travel routes actually available to the account. Travel co-payments and monthly redemption limits can mean a large balance cannot fund an entire planned trip.',
      'Use an equivalent cash booking rather than the highest displayed fare as the reference. Separate one-off joining points, milestone rewards and repeatable spending rewards. Do not assign one maximum rupee value to every point in every programme.'],
    ['Compare two accounts with the same spending schedule',
      'For an illustrative ₹10 lakh of eligible annual spending, a one-percentage-point incremental usable return is ₹10,000 before fees and caps. If the upgrade adds ₹13,570 tax-inclusive membership cost, rewards alone do not cover it under those assumptions. Planned benefits need to fill the gap; unused entitlements do not.',
      'Large spend can make fee-waiver conditions relevant, but only if the product’s eligible definition and period are met. Compare actual renewed terms rather than assuming an invitation, relationship status or annual total guarantees a specific premium variant.'],
    ['Keep the operational limits in the decision',
      'Check available credit, payment timing, statement controls and how a large purchase affects the next bill. A reward strategy must still be supported by cash to clear the statement. A high advertised return is not a reason to finance spending at a costly revolving rate.',
      'When a product or numerical model remains under review, use its documented conditions and reason for withheld estimates rather than inventing a rank. A manual product comparison can remain useful without claiming a precise net saving that the available inputs cannot establish.']
  ],
  'blog/rbi-credit-card-rules-2026': [
    ['Turn a disputed charge into a specific complaint',
      'Identify the transaction or fee, its date, the statement entry and the clause or offer you relied on. State the outcome sought, such as an explanation, correction or reversal. Attach the relevant record and preserve the complaint reference. This is more actionable than sending a general accusation that the whole statement is wrong.',
      'Keep merchant and issuer issues distinct. A merchant’s missing refund, an issuer’s interest calculation and an unauthorised transaction may require different evidence and processes. Do not assume raising one complaint automatically suspends all payment obligations. Ask how the disputed amount and undisputed dues will be treated while it is investigated.'],
    ['Read an EMI illustration as cash flows',
      'Before accepting a conversion, list the amount financed, instalments, interest, processing charges and any merchant discount. Compare with the non-EMI checkout price. A hypothetical ₹30,000 purchase paid through six ₹5,000 instalments plus a ₹500 processing charge costs at least ₹30,500 before any other charges. The label alone cannot make that fee disappear.',
      'Check whether ordinary rewards, an instant offer or a fee-waiver calculation treats the converted transaction differently. If the purchase is later returned, confirm cancellation of the EMI plan and the treatment of interest or fees separately from the merchant refund. Retain the conversion and cancellation confirmations.'],
    ['Keep closure records after the account is settled',
      'Before requesting closure, identify posted and pending purchases, instalment obligations, recurring services and unused rewards. Obtain an outstanding or settlement figure from the issuer where needed. Sending a payment does not by itself establish that an account has been closed.',
      'Keep the request acknowledgement, payment evidence and final closure confirmation. Check for a later statement or remaining entry and raise a specific query if the record differs. For a compensation request, identify the relevant rule and dates rather than counting from an assumed closure date.'],
    ['Separate account correction from a promised credit-score result',
      'A disputed account entry can be examined using the statement, issuer response and credit report. Ask for correction of the factual record and retain the resolution. Do not promise that a successful correction produces a particular score increase; a score reflects the wider credit file.',
      'Use official issuer support and the regulator’s published complaint process. Save reference numbers, dates and responses in one timeline. An accurate timeline helps establish the issue and escalation eligibility without relying on a social-media template that may describe different circumstances.']
  ],
};
